[{:db/id "q-21072/selection/e906ef29edeef4ecb3b785095064d0d5eb12d1a05cfceb1a51e817a699321600" :answer/id #uuid "393d7715-24e4-5ca5-b2f2-9a08e2ece75b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072/selection/cdb551f44b1c45bb27d018714d3e14abfaebbf2502cac528a0d4304c2c6285a9" :answer/id #uuid "d7d2fbeb-09bc-56bd-91f7-79d97c674678" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072/selection/a15d242c77b60dbfdb128f262bc2496f5dcbf6c56c01ace38f9e4dbb25845a9a" :answer/id #uuid "3bdc292d-2c36-5e29-8cb8-b1aa493c8278" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072/selection/1cdb58c82bebe77a5798d2b487c9c660e093504f158f23d69a1b3702869b5d9c" :answer/id #uuid "61bd84d2-4763-590b-be6a-188d4bf70820" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072/selection/e8b04e83a633927d5b17ec90f897a2a1b1a84a0313bd278653d3fdc8c888bbcc" :answer/id #uuid "7c7851e8-332f-58da-ae40-0cac35fae42d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d39a38dc-51bd-5b2b-80a3-191a3d1cbb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21072/selection/e8b04e83a633927d5b17ec90f897a2a1b1a84a0313bd278653d3fdc8c888bbcc" :answer-field/choices ["q-21072/selection/e906ef29edeef4ecb3b785095064d0d5eb12d1a05cfceb1a51e817a699321600" "q-21072/selection/cdb551f44b1c45bb27d018714d3e14abfaebbf2502cac528a0d4304c2c6285a9" "q-21072/selection/a15d242c77b60dbfdb128f262bc2496f5dcbf6c56c01ace38f9e4dbb25845a9a" "q-21072/selection/1cdb58c82bebe77a5798d2b487c9c660e093504f158f23d69a1b3702869b5d9c" "q-21072/selection/e8b04e83a633927d5b17ec90f897a2a1b1a84a0313bd278653d3fdc8c888bbcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21072" :question/id #uuid "62db0e45-63cb-50b4-b6ad-94c5afe9af6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21072" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-21072/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21072"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection/5cf32e6f33080f48fab42c1abe584b8e2baab8847ed4fb6264106505f2828df5" :answer/id #uuid "95649361-9e1f-5e27-b5c9-fd0151121add" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection/442f00d5a623d2106e4e1d9b220fdef6dd3c7d1177a49b137a3222f5bbe1bed7" :answer/id #uuid "d6ae750b-4381-548c-a5f1-4e7a3381e954" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection/6149254c8b9316a9d2547c86272af451238b7d865ee3e3217dadb072ec1eb2ba" :answer/id #uuid "41bd14a0-ea02-5ee2-8f12-d3bac387a819" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection/0032b3ed313b29560c02d29e919d1c6bb75456c9f1cc1b7e54dae4a8a39ad9c5" :answer/id #uuid "3dfc9689-98eb-560f-91e0-b3a60dc6de41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection/bec3306523c30042194c913fad6e0dcc3745bcf10ea45ae16fe55c7ebd66e1d5" :answer/id #uuid "fa908896-e17e-5612-b79e-9adbd1d99921" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c277f4-7b31-51c1-bc74-c46773e7decb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75278/selection/442f00d5a623d2106e4e1d9b220fdef6dd3c7d1177a49b137a3222f5bbe1bed7" :answer-field/choices ["q-75278/selection/5cf32e6f33080f48fab42c1abe584b8e2baab8847ed4fb6264106505f2828df5" "q-75278/selection/442f00d5a623d2106e4e1d9b220fdef6dd3c7d1177a49b137a3222f5bbe1bed7" "q-75278/selection/6149254c8b9316a9d2547c86272af451238b7d865ee3e3217dadb072ec1eb2ba" "q-75278/selection/0032b3ed313b29560c02d29e919d1c6bb75456c9f1cc1b7e54dae4a8a39ad9c5" "q-75278/selection/bec3306523c30042194c913fad6e0dcc3745bcf10ea45ae16fe55c7ebd66e1d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75278" :question/id #uuid "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75278" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75278/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75278"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection/9448ada8d052c355940fea09219b64af1f94b37363b53fdcc805f8be086814ad" :answer/id #uuid "6bfd5ed9-6475-5ec2-91d2-17a5f57bce7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection/2a7c1a99e9e23adaa901d3875aa3d18803784074d59c1f3334ed37864b04f786" :answer/id #uuid "9cefbaca-3550-55a0-b5a2-d5d79754501e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection/4de868faecbc009c790be5d03b9ee16bc248ff62e65dfe77f6a55baab2088b3f" :answer/id #uuid "24228cd6-c60b-57dc-8824-c0513896faae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection/8b5a8474a9b9934bc051275be5cb046d57f79f1810a453d2a6924c7b768a1f5e" :answer/id #uuid "7cf7e1e5-bcb8-5f04-bcc9-182d2b6580da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection/3c5e157e7f6663f1890b2b106ba57efe2f273c47b96853b2504b92d6602e697b" :answer/id #uuid "8c6df50d-74eb-5d93-9081-e20be9a88127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12c4a9ee-6329-5719-9169-2b8323892d0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122726/selection/2a7c1a99e9e23adaa901d3875aa3d18803784074d59c1f3334ed37864b04f786" :answer-field/choices ["q-122726/selection/9448ada8d052c355940fea09219b64af1f94b37363b53fdcc805f8be086814ad" "q-122726/selection/2a7c1a99e9e23adaa901d3875aa3d18803784074d59c1f3334ed37864b04f786" "q-122726/selection/4de868faecbc009c790be5d03b9ee16bc248ff62e65dfe77f6a55baab2088b3f" "q-122726/selection/8b5a8474a9b9934bc051275be5cb046d57f79f1810a453d2a6924c7b768a1f5e" "q-122726/selection/3c5e157e7f6663f1890b2b106ba57efe2f273c47b96853b2504b92d6602e697b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122726" :question/id #uuid "5b4d0b60-e3bd-5983-83d6-629fbc513ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122726" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122726/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122726"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection/6e8a839c6eec423c7d6db2056fd3bcbda1494940204b0f00ffce579859c42eb9" :answer/id #uuid "02652485-045b-5d46-94a8-23202aa3e5f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection/ed984aa0fc49964610796568215755fc9f2562b0ae2516bec0cd6da6d1a3dc4a" :answer/id #uuid "0c2402b6-aacf-5739-8974-6606432ca65a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection/a766fff8f49dab7622d4652d7fd56c6ab595db8631e1cd7ee65e4c4b733e15a9" :answer/id #uuid "1afebe6e-7606-5045-afe2-7d49c8b60693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection/70b0399788382c4195d0f74f53870690b62f45ebbc432f57640f5c928bc08fa7" :answer/id #uuid "783f33f1-7391-5ed6-8660-f05a8e52e776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection/95d9d84b954c110bc9ed54960f8ace44b300e810195ce882caa0fbb84d9034e6" :answer/id #uuid "43cd01f5-06ff-57b6-8751-bb864623a753" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed59925-3dd6-5551-ade5-4e6cbf1e3bc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122931/selection/70b0399788382c4195d0f74f53870690b62f45ebbc432f57640f5c928bc08fa7" :answer-field/choices ["q-122931/selection/6e8a839c6eec423c7d6db2056fd3bcbda1494940204b0f00ffce579859c42eb9" "q-122931/selection/ed984aa0fc49964610796568215755fc9f2562b0ae2516bec0cd6da6d1a3dc4a" "q-122931/selection/a766fff8f49dab7622d4652d7fd56c6ab595db8631e1cd7ee65e4c4b733e15a9" "q-122931/selection/70b0399788382c4195d0f74f53870690b62f45ebbc432f57640f5c928bc08fa7" "q-122931/selection/95d9d84b954c110bc9ed54960f8ace44b300e810195ce882caa0fbb84d9034e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122931" :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122931/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122931"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350395/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7d6cc217-9664-5d4f-8cf0-e616bf47e6e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350395/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "aae0949b-8861-50a2-8d79-737597369413" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350395/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-350395/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350395/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c3b448b6-88e6-5d8f-9478-834c71b11458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350395/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c3bb4b-951d-5aeb-86f0-accba0776ed0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350395/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-350395/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350395" :question/id #uuid "7ce811e3-7f18-52e4-8f6f-84b575e03283" :question/math-academy-id "q-350395" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-350395/field-1" "q-350395/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350395"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
